"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[63452],{63452:function(n){n.exports=JSON.parse('{"content":"\\n## Launching Activities via ADB\\nWhen developing on an Android device or emulator, there are times when you need to check the exact name of an installed package or find information about a specific screen (Activity).  \\nWe summarize how to check this through <mark>ADB (Android Debug Bridge)</mark> commands and launch the desired Activity directly.\\n### 1. Get ADB Root Permissions\\nFirst, obtain root permissions to run system commands smoothly.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Adb root command\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ adb root\\nadbd is already running as root\\n```\\n### 2. Check Installed Package List\\nYou can see a list of all packages installed on the system using the <mark>pm list packages</mark> command.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"List all installed packages\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ adb shell pm list packages\\n...\\npackage:com.android.bookmarkprovider\\n//[!code highlight]\\npackage:com.android.settings\\npackage:com.cyanogenmod.eleven\\npackage:com.android.calculator2\\npackage:org.lineageos.updater\\npackage:com.android.cts.ctsshim\\npackage:com.android.vpndialogs\\n...\\n```\\n### 3. Analyze Activity Information for Specific Packages\\nUse the <mark>resolve-activity</mark> command to check which Activity a specific component consists of, or what the intent filter information is.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Resolve activity for settings\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ adb shell cmd package resolve-activity --brief com.android.settings\\npriority=0 preferredOrder=0 match=0x108000 specificIndex=-1 isDefault=true\\n//[!code highlight]\\ncom.android.settings/.Settings\\n```\\n### 4. Launch Activity Directly\\nBased on the analyzed Activity information, you can use the <mark>am start</mark> command to launch the screen immediately.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Start activity directly\\"\\n}\\n---\\n//[!code word:com.android.settings/.Settings]\\n$ adb shell am start -n com.android.settings/.Settings\\nStarting: Intent { cmp=com.android.settings/.Settings }\\n```\\nUsing the <mark>package name/class name</mark> format as shown above allows you to launch not only the app\'s main screen but also specific settings screens exposed to the outside."}')}}]);